\mt1 Psalms

\c 5

\s1 Listen to Me at Dawn

\d For the choirmaster. On the *nechilot*. A psalm of David.\f + \fr 5:0 \fk KONTEXT \fq For the choirmaster. On the nechilot \ft The Hebrew superscription has two musical directions. *Lamnaṣṣēaḥ* — "for the choirmaster" or "to the music director" — names the leader of the temple's musical performance, the same term that opens Psalm 4. *El-hannəḥîlôt* is harder. *Nəḥîlôt* is a hapax — a word that appears only here in the entire Hebrew Bible — and its meaning is uncertain. Most scholars take it as a wind-instrument direction, probably reed pipes used in lament liturgy: "on the flutes." The Greek translation (the Septuagint) read it differently, hearing the consonants *n-ḥ-l* as related to *naḥalāh*, "inheritance," and rendered the line *hyper tês klēronomousēs*, "for the one who inherits." On this Greek reading the Midrash Tehillim reads the psalm allegorically as Israel's morning Torah-study petition. Both readings have shaped the psalm's reception, but the Hebrew original most likely names a real instrument that real musicians played at the second Temple. — Psalm 5 belongs to a deliberate four-psalm sequence at the opening of Book One: Psalm 3 is morning, Psalm 4 is evening, Psalm 5 is morning, Psalm 6 is night. The Psalter's editors arranged these so that the reader walks through a full day of the soul: the morning fight (Ps 3), the evening peace (Ps 4), the dawn court (Ps 5), the late-night tears (Ps 6). This psalm is the second morning prayer in that cycle — and unlike Psalm 3, which is a battlefield prayer, this one is a courtroom prayer.\f*\f + \fr 5:0 \fk TEXT \fq A psalm of David \ft *Mizmôr lə-dāwid* in Hebrew. The *lamed* ("of/to/for") is grammatically open: it can mean "composed by David," "dedicated to David," "in the tradition of David," or "from the Davidic collection." Most modern scholarship reads the Davidic ascriptions in Book One (Pss 3–41) as collection-designations rather than authorship claims. The voice in this psalm is anyone who has been slandered and has nowhere left to go but the temple at first light.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 1 Listen to my words, LORD —\f + \fr 5:1 \fk KULTUR \fq what I cannot say \ft The Hebrew word is *hăgîg*, and it appears as a noun only here and in Psalm 39:4. It does not mean "meditation," which is the rendering most older English Bibles give. *Hăgîg* comes from the root *hgh*, the same root used in Isaiah 31:4 for the growl of a lion and in Isaiah 38:14 for the mourning of a dove. It is the body's pre-verbal sound — the moan that has not yet become speech, the sigh that contains everything the speaker cannot say. The psalm opens with a deliberate descent: from *ʾămāray* (my articulate words) to *hăgîgî* (the wordless sound underneath them) to *qôl šawʿî* (my cry for help) in the next verse. The speaker is losing language as the prayer proceeds, falling toward the body. The German feminist Old Testament scholar Ulrike Bail (*Gegen das Schweigen klagen*, 2005) has read this opening as the psalm of those who have been silenced — whose words have been taken from them by violence or trauma — and who must begin again from the moan. The psalm's first line legitimizes prayer that has not yet found language. In Romans 8:26 Paul names the same thing: "the Spirit intercedes with sighs too deep for words." Modern therapy culture has rediscovered what this psalm assumes — that the body knows before the language does, and the sound the body makes is already a form of speech.\f*
\q1 and to what I cannot say.

\b
\q1
\v 2 Hear my cry,
\q1 my King and my God —\f + \fr 5:2 \fk KULTUR \fq my King and my God \ft The address is patron language. In the ancient Near Eastern world, a person under threat appealed to a powerful patron — the local king, the regional governor, the temple authority — by claiming the patron as *theirs*: "my king." The claim was not affectionate possession. It was the formal invocation of a relationship in which the patron was honor-bound to hear the client. To fail to hear was to lose face publicly. Hossfeld and Zenger note that this address — combining the political title *malkî* (my King) with the relational title *ʾĕlōhāy* (my God) — situates the entire psalm inside a courtroom logic of patronage and obligation. The speaker is not flattering. The speaker is binding the hearer. — The verb chain in vv. 1–2 also descends from articulate speech to wordless cry to formed petition: "my words" → "what I cannot say" → "the sound of my cry for help" → "I bring my prayer." The Hebrew *šawʿ* in this verse is not a generic prayer-word; it is the urgent help-cry, the sound a person makes calling for the police, the sound of someone trapped. The speaker is rising back up from the moan into addressed speech.\f*
\q1 to you I bring my prayer.

\b
\q1
\v 3 In the morning you will hear my voice.\f + \fr 5:3 \fk TEXT \fq In the morning... in the morning... I lay out my case... and I watch \ft Three details in this verse together establish the psalm's setting and have to be held in mind for the rest of it to make sense. — First, the doubled *bōqer* ("morning… morning…"). The repetition is structural, not poetic flourish. It marks the dawn bracket of a formal religious-legal act: at first light, before the day's juridical activity, the petitioner goes to the temple. Most English translations collapse the two "mornings" into one. The Hebrew preserves them deliberately. — Second, the verb *ʿārak*. It does not mean "pray." *ʿĀrak* is forensic vocabulary — "to lay out, arrange, set in order" — used for marshaling troops (2 Samuel 10:9), setting a table (Psalm 23:5), and laying out a legal case before a judge. The psalmist is not informing God of a feeling. The psalmist is filing a brief. — Third, the verb *ṣāpâ* — "to watch, to keep watch" — sentinel posture. The waiting that follows the petition is active, not passive. The watchman on the wall expects to see a verdict-oracle arrive. — Hans-Joachim Kraus and Sigmund Mowinckel both classified Psalm 5 as a "prayer of the accused" — a person who has been slandered, who has nowhere to bring the case in human courts, and who appeals at dawn to the divine judge for vindication. The closest contemporary analogue is the emergency injunction filed first thing Monday morning before the legal day begins.\f*
\q1 In the morning I lay out my case before you,
\q1 and I watch.

\b
\q1
\v 4 You are not the kind of God\f + \fr 5:4 \fk TEXT \fq You are not the kind of God who delights in wickedness \ft Verses 4–6 are written in the form of a *negative entrance liturgy* — a list of who God is *not* and who therefore cannot enter God's presence. The form has a parallel in Psalm 15:1–2 ("Who may dwell on your holy hill?") and in the Egyptian *Negative Confession* of the Book of the Dead, where the deceased declares before forty-two judges all the wrongs they have *not* committed. The Hebrew biblical version inverts the structure: the speaker is not declaring their own innocence but God's character — God is the kind of being incompatible with evil. The architecture matters because verse 7 will pivot on it: *I* will enter — by *ḥesed*, not by qualifying as innocent under the entrance liturgy. — *Ḥāpēṣ* ("delights in") is a strong verb. It is the same word the Song of Songs uses for erotic delight (7:11) and Genesis uses for Shechem's desire for Dinah (34:19). It does not mean "approve." It names what God's affections do and do not do.\f*
\q1 who delights in wickedness.
\q1 Evil cannot stay with you.

\b
\q1
\v 5 The arrogant cannot stand before your eyes.
\q1 You hate everyone who deals in harm.\f + \fr 5:5 \fk UNGLAUBLICH \fq you hate \ft The verb is *śānēʾ*. It is not softened in the Hebrew, and it should not be softened in the translation. The psalm says God *hates*. — Three honest readings of this. (1) *Critical-naturalistic*: The line is the moral world of an ancient Near Eastern lament writer who needs the divine judge to hate the right people. It is the same moral logic that runs through every imprecatory psalm and through much of the Hebrew Bible's prophetic literature, and it is uncomfortable for a modern reader who has inherited a different theology of universal divine love. The line should be read for what it is — a witness to the ancient world's God, not as ethical instruction. (2) *Symbolic-theological*: Hebrew "love" and "hate" are often dispositional and covenantal rather than emotional. To "hate" in this register means *to be in fundamental incompatibility with* — a relational orientation, not an emotional state. On this reading God does not hate persons but the orientation toward harm-production; the divine being is incompatible with what destroys what God has made. The biblical philosopher Abraham Joshua Heschel (*The Prophets*, 1962) developed the doctrine of divine *pathos*: God's affections are real and engaged, not impassive, but they target what wounds creation, not arbitrary persons. (3) *Historical-believing*: Read in the line of Christian and Jewish reception, the verse names what holiness costs. A God who could co-exist indifferently with the destruction of the innocent would be a God not worth worshipping. The line is the necessary backside of the next verse: the same God who hates the destruction of the slandered is the God who is committed to the slandered. — The psalm itself does not resolve the question. It places the line on the page and trusts the reader to live with it.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 6 You destroy the liars.
\q1 The bloodthirsty deceiver — the LORD detests him.\f + \fr 5:6 \fk KULTUR \fq the bloodthirsty deceiver \ft The Hebrew is *ʾîš-dāmîm ûmirmāh* — literally "a man of bloods and deceit." Modern English readers often hear "bloodthirsty" and "deceitful" as two different kinds of bad people. The Hebrew identifies them as the same person. The slanderer kills with words. False testimony in the ancient Israelite legal system could result in execution, exile, or social annihilation; deceitful speech and bloodshed were not parallel evils but the same evil seen from two angles. The Latin American liberation theologian Elsa Tamez (*Bible of the Oppressed*, 1982) reads this as the recurring social pattern named throughout the Psalter: those who hold social power destroy the marginalized through speech — by lying about them, by bringing false charges, by saying what is needed to ruin a name. The psalmist is the target of exactly this. The next visible image of the slanderer comes in v. 9: *their throat — an open grave*.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 7 But I —\f + \fr 5:7 \fk TEXT \fq But I — by your great steadfast love — will enter your house \ft This is the theological hinge of the entire psalm. — The Hebrew opens with a contrastive *waw* ("but") plus an emphatic pronoun *ʾănî* ("I"). The structure is unmistakable: *but I, in contrast to all those just excluded, I am the one who enters*. The hinge is grammatical and tonal — the door of vv. 4–6 is closed, and v. 7 is the door opening. The translation must not soften it; "and I" or "as for me" loses the contrast. — *Bĕrōb ḥasdĕkā* is the load-bearing prepositional phrase. *Ḥesed* is one of the central words of the Hebrew Bible and one of the hardest to translate. It is not "mercy" (which is *raḥămîm*, a different word) and not generic "kindness." *Ḥesed* names the loyalty one party keeps to another after they have entered into covenant — a sustained, unwillingness-to-let-go love that continues even when the other party has failed. William Tyndale coined "lovingkindness" for it in 1535. Contemporary scholarship has settled on "steadfast love" as the closest English rendering. — The theological claim is what makes the verse a hinge: access to God's house is *not* by the speaker's righteousness, *not* by qualifying under the entrance liturgy of vv. 4–6, *not* by merit. Access is by *God's* covenant-loyalty, not the speaker's character. Jon D. Levenson (*The Love of God*, 2016) calls this the structural logic of the entire Psalter — *ḥesed* is primarily a divine attribute that precedes and grounds every human approach. Modern readers raised on either secular meritocracy ("what did you do to deserve this?") or religious meritocracy ("are you good enough to be heard?") tend to read the verse the wrong way around. The psalm is doing something stranger: the speaker enters because the One inside has bound himself in covenant to people in trouble.\f*
\q1 by your great steadfast love —
\q1 will enter your house.\f + \fr 5:7 \fk KONTEXT \fq will enter your house \ft "Your house" is the Jerusalem temple, and the entry is not metaphorical. In ancient Israelite legal practice, the temple functioned as a sanctuary for the falsely accused — a court of last resort when human justice had failed or could not be reached. Deuteronomy 17:8–13 and 1 Kings 8:31–32 describe the formal procedure: a person under accusation comes to the temple, lays out the case, and waits for divine judgment. Hans-Joachim Kraus (*Psalms 1–59*, 1988, 153–155) traces this category of Hebrew prayer — the *Gebet des Angeklagten*, "prayer of the accused" — and argues that Psalm 5 belongs to it. The temple is not a place of devotion in this verse. It is a courthouse. The speaker is filing for asylum.\f*\f + \fr 5:7 \fk LEBEN \fq by your great steadfast love \ft The line is the corrective for one of the most persistent religious distortions: that you must qualify before God will hear you. — Most spiritual formation traditions agree that the entry-by-merit instinct dies hard, even after a person has theologically rejected it. Catholic *examen* (Ignatius), Reformed assurance (Calvin), Jewish high holiday liturgy, Quaker silent meeting — different traditions have developed different practices, but the work is recognizably the same: training the speaker to approach God as one who is *already loved by covenant*, not as one who must first prove worth. — The psalmist's words are precise. *I, by your great steadfast love, will enter your house*. Not "if I'm worthy." Not "if I'm good enough today." Not "if I have prayed enough this week." The grammar is causal: the entry is caused by *God's* loyalty, not the speaker's. Anyone who has lived inside the experience of perfectionism, religious or secular, recognizes the difference. The psalm is a daily form of prayer that breaks the meritocracy.\f*
\q1 I will bow toward your holy temple,
\q1 in awe of you.

\b
\q1
\v 8 LORD, lead me in your justice\f + \fr 5:8 \fk KULTUR \fq in your justice \ft The Hebrew is *bĕṣidqātekā* — "in your *ṣĕdāqâ*," from the root *ṣ-d-q*. As in Psalm 4:1, the word does not name an abstract moral attribute. *Ṣedeq* is the rightness God owes to the slandered, the poor, and the wrongly accused — what an honest judge does for someone whose case has been buried by the powerful. To pray "lead me in your justice" is not to ask for moral coaching. It is to ask the divine judge to vindicate publicly someone whose name is being destroyed in the social court of the community. The phrase is parallel to v. 3's *I lay out my case*: the petition is to be led, in this case, all the way to acquittal.\f*
\q1 because of those who watch me.\f + \fr 5:8 \fk LEBEN \fq those who watch me \ft *Šôrĕrāy* in Hebrew is a participle from a verb that means "to watch, to lie in wait, to spy." These are not enemies in general — they are surveillance enemies. People who are watching, looking for a misstep, ready to weaponize what they see. — The contemporary resonance is sharper than the ancient one in some ways. To live with the awareness that someone is watching for the wrong move — at work, in public, online — is the daily experience of many people, often disproportionately of women, of public figures, of people from marginalized communities. The psalm does not deny that the watching is real. It asks for a level path through it. *Make your way clear before me*: not "make the watchers go away," but "do not let me stumble in front of them." The prayer assumes the surveillance is not going to stop, and asks for steadiness in spite of it.\f*
\q1 Make your way clear before me.

\b
\q1
\v 9 Nothing in their mouth can stand.\f + \fr 5:9 \fk KULTUR \fq their throat — an open grave \ft The Hebrew is *qeber-pāṯûaḥ gĕrônām*. The structure is a *nominal clause of identification*, not a simile. Their throat IS an open grave. Most English translations soften this to "their throats are *like* open graves," but the Hebrew is doing something stranger and harder: an equation. The slanderer's throat is identified with the grave because what comes out of it produces the same result. — There is a deep ancient Near Eastern background. In Ugaritic mythology (recovered from clay tablets at Ras Shamra in 1928), the death-god Mot is characterized by an enormous throat that swallows the dead: the grave personified. Mitchell Dahood read v. 9 against this Ugaritic background and argued that the Hebrew is doing demythologization — taking the imagery that pagan religion attached to a death-deity and applying it to the destructive speakers within Israelite society. The wicked are not afraid of Mot. *They are Mot.* — The Latin American liberation theologian Elsa Tamez and the German feminist scholar Ulrike Bail both read this verse as the psalmist's most concrete sociological statement: false speech is killing speech. False testimony in the ancient Israelite legal system could result in real death, exile, or total social erasure. The verse is not metaphor with extra emphasis. It is naming a real political phenomenon — destructive speech as the death-dealing power of the powerful — that the modern reader recognizes from any era's slander campaigns and disinformation cycles.\f*
\q1 Inside them: ruin.
\q1 Their throat — an open grave.
\q1 Their tongue: smooth.\f + \fr 5:9 \fk KONTEXT \fq their throat — an open grave \ft Paul quotes this verse in Romans 3:13 — *"Their throat is an open grave; with their tongues they have used deceit"* — as part of a chain of Old Testament citations he assembles to argue for universal human sinfulness. Paul's use is rhetorical, not exegetical: he stitches the line together with phrases from Psalms 14, 36, and 140 and Isaiah 59 to build a theological case in his letter, not to expound Psalm 5 on its own terms. — On Psalm 5's own terms, the verse is doing something narrower than Paul's universal-sin reading. The "they" of v. 9 is not "all human beings"; it is the specific class of slanderers whose destructive speech is killing the speaker. Modern Jewish reading consistently takes the verse this way: it is not a doctrine about humanity but a description of a particular social violence the speaker is experiencing. — Both readings can stand. Paul is quoting the line as a proof-text in a theological argument; the psalm is using the line to name an opponent. Christian readers who know the verse only from Romans 3 will hear the psalm differently when they meet it in its first home — as the speech of a victim, not a description of all humans.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 10 Find them guilty, God.\f + \fr 5:10 \fk KONTEXT \fq find them guilty, God... let them fall by their own plans \ft Verses 10–11 are the imprecatory passage of the psalm — the petition for the destruction of enemies — and they are the most theologically dissonant lines for many modern Western Christian readers. Three things have to be held together to read them honestly. — First, *the imprecation is transferred to God*. The psalmist is not picking up a weapon. The grammar of v. 10 is a series of imperatives addressed to God: *find them guilty, let them fall, drive them out*. Hossfeld and Zenger describe this as the psalm's renunciation of private vengeance: the speaker hands the case over rather than acting on the anger. The pattern matters because it makes the imprecation a different kind of speech-act than retaliation. — Second, *the destruction is intrinsic*. "Let them fall *by their own plans*" is not a request for external violence. The psalmist is asking God to let the destructive plans destroy the planners — the deceit collapses on the deceivers, the lies poison the liars. This is closer to a prayer for the moral physics of the world to operate than to a prayer for divine torture. — Third, *the imprecatory psalms are resistance literature*. The Latin American liberation theologian Elsa Tamez (*Bible of the Oppressed*, 1982) and the African American theologian James Cone (*The Spirituals and the Blues*) both read the imprecatory psalms as the prayer of the powerless when human courts have failed. They are not the prayer of the powerful asking permission to destroy enemies. Martin Luther King Jr.'s sermon "When Peace Becomes Obnoxious" (1956) names the same theological move: praying for justice when no human institution will deliver it. The verse is unbearable when it is in the mouth of an oppressor. It is necessary when it is in the mouth of the slandered. — The closing line is the load-bearing one: *for they have rebelled against you*. The petition is justified not by personal injury but by the slanderers' rebellion against God. The case is God's case, not the speaker's.\f*
\q1 Let them fall by their own plans.
\q1 For their many crimes, drive them out —
\q1 for they have rebelled against you.

\b
\q1
\v 11 But let everyone who takes shelter in you rejoice.\f + \fr 5:11 \fk TEXT \fq everyone who takes shelter in you \ft The Hebrew word is *ḥōseh*, from the root *ḥ-s-h*, "to seek refuge, to take shelter." The same root produces the noun *maḥseh*, the standard Psalter word for God as refuge (Psalm 46:1, Psalm 91:2). This is the psalm's communal expansion: from the singular speaker who has been laying out a case to *all* who take shelter — the whole community of the threatened. The architecture of vv. 11–12 is intentional: the speaker, having been heard, immediately widens the petition outward to include everyone in the same situation. — The verbs that follow are joy-verbs. *Yāśam* (rejoice). *Yərannēn* (sing out, ring out — a high-pitched joy-cry, not quiet contentment). *Yaʿloṣ* (be jubilant). The dawn that began in moan ends in shout. The arc from v. 1 (*hăgîg* — wordless moan) to v. 11 (*yərannēnû* — full-throated joy-cry) is the psalm's emotional spine: from sub-verbal pain to super-verbal joy.\f*
\q1 Let them shout for joy forever.
\q1 Spread your shelter over them.
\q1 Let those who love your name be loud with joy in you.

\b
\q1
\v 12 For you bless the righteous, LORD.\f + \fr 5:12 \fk TEXT \fq like a great shield \ft The Hebrew is *kaṣṣinnāh* — "like the *ṣinnâ*." Most English translations render this as "shield," which is correct as far as it goes but loses a specific image. *Ṣinnâ* is one of two Hebrew words for shield, and the distinction matters. *Māgēn* is the small round buckler — the round shield Goliath's armor-bearer carried in 1 Samuel 17:7, the size of a personal defensive weapon. *Ṣinnâ* is the large rectangular body-shield, the equivalent of the Roman *scutum* — full-body coverage, used by the front line of an army to protect the soldiers behind. When 1 Kings 10:16 lists Solomon's gold shields, it distinguishes the two: 200 *ṣinnāh*-shields (large), 300 *māgēn*-shields (small). The psalm's closing image is not a personal-sized shield. It is the wide royal shield extended over the protected one — total coverage. — The verb in the line is *ṭāʿar*, from *ʿāṭar* — "to crown," the regal verb for placing a crown on a head. God's *rāṣôn* (favor, goodwill, divine pleasure) is what God uses to crown the righteous. The image binds two royal vocabularies together: the wide protective shield of a king's bodyguard and the crown of a king's coronation. The psalm closes not with the speaker hiding from harm, but with the speaker crowned by the same favor that protects them.\f*
\q1 Like a great shield,
\q1 your favor crowns them.
